  First question for sub-project one, and it's the one everything else hangs off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether tasks are shared between people, or each person has their own.

  - [1mCollaborative / shared tasks[0m — multiple people see and edit the same tasks. This is the reading your original
    request implies: "tasks they care about change" usually means [3msomeone else[0m changed it. Requires real accounts, a
    server, and an authorization model from day one. Nothing about it is cheap to retrofit.
  - [1mPersonal tasks, one user[0m — no sharing, no accounts, browser storage is enough. Cheap and fast, but it hollows out
    notifications: the only thing that can change your task is you, so a notification is at most a reminder. If this is
    the answer, "notifications" likely means [3mscheduled reminders[0m, which is a genuinely different subsystem than the one
    you asked for.
  - [1mPersonal tasks, synced across devices[0m — one user, several devices. Needs a server and accounts, but no sharing or
    permissions. Notifications become real ("changed on your laptop, notify your phone") without the complexity of
    multi-party permissions.

  What's cheap to change later: the UI, the notification channels, how tasks are styled. What is [1mnot[0m: whether there's a
  server, and whether the data model assumes one owner or many participants. Both of those are load-bearing for every
  later decision, so I'd rather get them right than get them fast.

  [1mMy recommendation: collaborative / shared tasks[0m — but only because your phrasing points there. "Tasks they care about"
  implies watching something you don't solely control. If I've over-read that, say so; personal-with-sync is
  meaningfully simpler and I'd rather build the simpler correct thing than the more impressive wrong one.
